Marathon 2 was written by Bungie Software <http://www.bungie.com/> and released
as source in 1999.

Aleph One is the engine built from that source. Its enhancements and extensions
to Marathon 2 were made by Loren Petrich, Chris Pruett, Ian Pitcher, Rhys Hill,
Ben Thompson, Mihai Parparita, Bo Lindbergh and others.
<https://github.com/Aleph-One-Marathon/alephone>

The SDL port of Aleph One was made by Christian Bauer
<Christian.Bauer@uni-mainz.de>.

The original Dreamcast port, AlephOne-0.12.0-dc-1 (2002), was made by BERO
against KallistiOS 1.1.7, and released through sdl-dc.sourceforge.net.

This version -- the modern toolchain, VMU saved games, the controller interface
and the PowerVR renderer -- is by Max Gibbons, with Claude (Anthropic).

Aleph One uses the Expat XML library developed by James Clark.

Vendored under dc/vendor, both MIT and both by Falco Girgis
<https://github.com/gyrovorbis>:

  sh4zam        SH4 store-queue and math routines
  VMU Profiler  the framerate readout on the memory card's screen

Built with KallistiOS <https://github.com/KallistiOS/KallistiOS>, kos-ports,
GLdc <https://gitlab.com/simulant/GLdc> and mkdcdisc
<https://gitlab.com/simulant/mkdcdisc>.
